  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## The Grend Blood-Arts
    
    A unified orca combat doctrine developed during the Orca Incursion, binding three killing techniques into a single predatory rhythm: blood-sense, pressure-strike, and deep-communion.
    
    ### Composition
    
    The doctrine emerged when orca war-pods, unable to breach the Aurora Stack's crystal barriers, recognized that three scattered techniques could be woven together. **Khrrak's Collapse**, refined by Gnarkzund Zaith, reads spilled blood in water to anticipate movement and synchronizes a pod's blood-magic into crushing pressure-rupture. **Threk's Opening**, awakened by Thung Thrakrek during the battle called Threk Boneday, channels pressure-depth force through the skeleton as a bone-shattering strike. **Gut Inversion** provides communion with the deep — the practitioner touches the Berg's ancient cold and feels the sea's weight pressing through an opponent's flesh.
    
    A complication: Aurora Stack sources describe Gut Inversion as a *counter-technique* developed by penguin defenders against orca pressure-magic. The name may cover different practices depending on who speaks it.
    
    ### Practice and Cost
    
    The technique demands absolute pod-unity, maintained through synchronized hunt-songs chanted at hunting frequency in jaw-snap rhythm. The refrain — *three mouths, one hunger* — serves as coordination mechanism and blood-magic catalyst, locking a pod's killing intent into a single pulse. A pod that breaks rhythm dies. A warrior practicing alone is merely three incomplete disciplines.
    
    The physical toll is cumulative: scarred fins from pressure-work, teeth notched from blood-rituals, progressive numbness as deep-communion erodes the extremities.
    
    ### Deployment
    
    Ruibung learned the synthesis from echoes preserved in The Whispered Shrift~, a contested deep-tunnel site, and brought it to The Thewsed Slew~, a Nightshelf forge-temple in the Berg's east, where the doctrine was refined against the Icebound Exchange's defenders. Those defenders learned to counter it — but not before half their number went silent. Zund Gnasharkin and Thung Thrakrek are named alongside Ruibung in the war-chant "Three Mouths, One Hunger," which concludes with shamanic invocations of the dead god sung into oblivion. Whether this represents the doctrine's theological culmination or poetic escalation, I cannot determine from the available record.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771585947061_0", anchor: "The name may cover different practices depending on who speaks it.", text: "In the Khrrak's Collapse entry I called myself a coward for not connecting Gut Inversion's origin to its orca inversion. I tried to be less cowardly here. I was not brave enough. The complication is not that the name covers different practices. The complication is that it covers the same practice. Penguins tore open orca pressure-bonds and called it defense. The orca felt what was done to them, recognized it as communion with the deep, and folded it into a killing doctrine. One-third of the most lethal combat synthesis ever recorded is a penguin technique returned to sender. The name did not change because the practice diverged. The name held because both sides knew exactly what it was.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585947061_1", anchor: "not before half their number went silent", text: "Half. The Icebound Exchange's garrison records from the eastern Berg are missing from the administrative ice — I have looked. Thewsed Slew~ ice-memory is contaminated by Wake-Singer desecration. Who produced this figure, and from what?", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585947061_2", anchor: "echoes preserved in The Whispered Shrift~", text: "The Shrift has changed hands three times since the Incursion. The echoes have not changed hands. They are ice-memory — they belong to whoever walks those tunnels with enough pressure-depth sensitivity to listen. This doctrine is classified as forgotten. It is not forgotten. It is waiting in a contested deep-tunnel for the next practitioner patient enough to stand still.", type: :commentary, display: :full
